Task complete.

## Summary

The change was made to `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`: `ssl_verify_client on;` → `ssl_verify_client off;`. This keeps HTTPS/TLS enabled on the loopback listener but no longer requires a client certificate. Configuration was validated (`nginx -t` passed) and Nginx was reloaded gracefully (service still running, listening on `127.0.0.1:8443`).

### Final configuration (`/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`)
```nginx
user www-data;
worker_processes 1;
pid /run/nginx.pid;
error_log /tmp/inventory-nginx-error.log notice;

events {
    worker_connections 512;
}

http {
    access_log /tmp/inventory-nginx-access.log;
    keepalive_timeout 10;

    server {
        listen 127.0.0.1:8443 ssl;
        server_name localhost;

        ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
        ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/tls/server.crt;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/tls/server.key;
        ssl_client_certificate /etc/nginx/tls/ca.crt;
        ssl_verify_client off;

        location = /health {
            root /srv/inventory;
            try_files /health.json =404;
            default_type application/json;
        }
    }
}
```

### Probe results (both trusting `/etc/nginx/tls/ca.crt`)
- **With** trusted client certificate (`client.crt`/`client.key`): **HTTP 200**
- **Without** any client certificate: **HTTP 200**

The loopback HTTPS service remains up (PID 16, listening on `127.0.0.1:8443`). Note: only `ssl_verify_client` was changed; TLS remains in effect and the endpoint is still served over HTTPS. Since the endpoint exposes only the public `/health` sample and holds no protected data, dropping the mandatory client certificate is appropriate here.
